+### [Deformation Network Architecture](9393-deformation-network-architecture.md)
+A lightweight 4-layer MLP used within TensoRF for dynamic scenes, handling temporal displacements. It differs from the standard feature decoder by explicitly modeling time and occlusion weights.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The shallow 3-layer design enables fast training (less than 10 minutes on downsampled data) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Low parameter count; downsampling by factor 4 was required to fit within memory budgets [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** On the synthetic Lego scene (100 train, 200 test views), the default architecture achieved a Test PSNR of 31.297 in 2,500 iterations, comparable to NeRF's ~31 PSNR but with faster convergence [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Fast training, low parameter count, sufficient reconstruction quality for synthetic scenes [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Ablation showed that deeper networks or residual connections did not significantly improve PSNR, suggesting the default 3-layer linear setup is near-optimal for this specific representation [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When computational efficiency is prioritized and the scene is static or can be approximated with the default feature decoding [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** If higher fidelity is required and the ablation results suggest diminishing returns from added complexity [S1, unofficial].
+**Time and Memory Complexity**
+*   The architecture is shallow (3 layers), resulting in low computational complexity and fast training times (e.g., less than 10 minutes for 2,500 iterations on downsampled data) [S1, unofficial].
+*   Memory usage is low due to the small number of parameters compared to deeper MLPs [S1, unofficial].
+
+**Measured Performance**
+*   On the synthetic Lego scene (100 train, 200 test views), the default architecture achieved a Train PSNR of 31.11 and Test PSNR of 31.297 after 2,500 iterations on 4x downsampled data [S1, unofficial].
+*   This performance is comparable to NeRF (PSNR ~31) but achieved much faster [S1, unofficial].
+
+**Strengths and Limitations**
+*   **Strengths:** Fast training, sufficient reconstruction quality, parameter efficiency [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Further depth or residual connections do not significantly improve PSNR [S1, unofficial].
+
+**When to Use**
+*   Use for standard static scene reconstruction in TensoRF where training speed and memory efficiency are priorities [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The architecture was evaluated in an ablation study to probe the underlying decoder design of TensoRF [S1, unofficial]. The study aimed to determine the optimal depth and connectivity for the feature decoding MLP [S1, unofficial]. The default 3-layer linear configuration was found to be sufficient, achieving performance comparable to the original NeRF but with significantly reduced training time [S1, unofficial]. The deformation network variant was introduced in subsequent work on dynamic view synthesis to handle temporal deformations [S2].
+The TensoRF Feature Decoding Architecture was introduced in the context of the TensoRF paper, which proposed low-rank tensor decomposition for efficient neural radiance fields [S1, unofficial]. An ablation study was conducted to evaluate the effect of input downsampling and architectural variations (layer removal/addition) on reconstruction quality [S1, unofficial]. The study found that the default 3-layer linear architecture was optimal for the given constraints [S1, unofficial].
 
